      "You do not choose this; it follows who you are assigning to, so the form works it out and tells you which applies and why. Until you have picked an assignee it cannot say, because the answer depends on your relationship to that person. Assigning inside your own reporting line gives you a Budget: you set how much working time the job is worth, and how that time is spent and re-planned is settled between the assignee and their own management. A Budget is about the allocation of effort — it is not an invitation to renegotiate the deadline. Assigning outside your reporting line gives you a Deadline: there is no shared management line to settle allocation along, so the assigning side owns the date outright. What decides this is the reporting line and nothing else — a department boundary does not change it. If you manage someone who sits in another department, they are still inside your line and you still get a Budget; if you share a department with someone who does not report to you, you do not. Each relationship allows exactly one of the two, so there is no control to switch between them — changing who the work is for is what changes the model. The new task form tells you which applies before you create it, and the task itself repeats it afterwards, so the assignee can see why their deadline works the way it does without having to ask the person who set it. When a time budget reaches you, you have two answers and neither is a refusal: accept it, which sets the deadline and makes the task yours to start; or propose the time you need instead, which goes back to the person who set it for approval. You cannot decline a task because of the hours — the task stays exactly where it is while your proposal is decided, and the exchange is kept on the task's deadline history. Declining the assignment itself is a separate thing with its own control. Two tasks have neither. A task you have broken down into subtasks keeps its deadline — that date is the ceiling every subtask sits under — but shows no time budget of its own, because each subtask carries its own and is negotiated with the person doing it. A goal-linked task has none either, and is never asked for hours: its time lives on its roadmap, one deadline per step, and each step earns its points only if it is handed in by that step's date. What a goal is asked for instead is a goal statement and a target date — the date the whole goal is aimed at, which is shown on the roadmap but is not itself scored. **When the budget is accepted, the deadline runs from when you could first have started — not from when you pressed accept.** On an ordinary task that is the moment you first came online at or after the task was given: online since before it arrived, the clock starts when it arrived; came online later, it starts when you did. Sitting on an acceptance while online does not move your deadline later — the wait was yours. If you accept while not Online, the clock starts at the acceptance itself, because that press is the first moment the system can prove you were there. On a cross-department task it starts when the receiving manager granted the hours, since nothing could begin before that. The date this produces can already be in the past — accept late enough and the task arrives Overdue, which is the rule working, not a fault. The anchor is stamped on the task once, when the budget is accepted, and does not move afterwards. **The task shows you which moment it counted from.** Open History under the time budget and the first line reads “Counted from 16 Aug · 15:33 IST”, so the date is never a bare number you have to take on trust. It sits with the budget workings rather than under the deadline, which stays a single clean date. A task created before this was recorded shows nothing there.",
