      "Who may do it: the person who ASSIGNED the task, or a TL or CEO. It is not a question of job title — a manager whose role is recorded as employee can still send back work they assigned, because the right comes from having assigned it. Anyone else is refused with “Only this task's assigner, or a TL/CEO, can send it back for rework.” The same rule covers waiving the rework deduction and deciding a deadline extension, because those are parts of the same review: whoever may approve the work may also send it back, and the two halves are never split. **Rejection is not currently offered.** The review panel shows two choices, Approve and Rework — the Reject button was hidden on 16 Aug 2026 by the owner's decision. Nothing behind it was removed: tasks rejected before that still show their rejection and its reason, the score still reads those records, and the separate Reject on an approval — an effort estimate, a deadline extension — is a different control and is unaffected. What follows describes rejection as it works when it is offered. Rework sends the task back to be redone and it returns to in progress; you resubmit when it is ready. Rejection is a decision that the submission is not acceptable. Both are recorded events rather than status changes alone, because the score reads how work was completed, not only whether it was. A reviewer must give a reason to reject. Sending work back also requires naming what is wrong with it: the reviewer ticks the completion requirements that were not met, and cannot send it back without ticking at least one. **On a subtask the list has two groups** — the subtask's own requirements, and below them the project requirements it was created to answer, under the heading “From the project”. Either kind can be ticked, because “you have not satisfied this project requirement” is as real a reason to send work back as a criterion of its own; the engine accepts both and refuses anything that is neither — the message is \u201CSelect at least one completion requirement that needs correction.\u201D They can add optional correction notes and reference files alongside. You then see a \u201CChanges requested\u201D panel on the task listing exactly those requirements and nothing else, with the note and any files beneath it. Every round is kept: once you resubmit, the panel stops warning you but the history of what was asked for stays on the task, so a second or third round never erases the first. A task with no completion requirements at all can still be sent back, because there is nothing to tick. **Rework gives you back the time you had left when you handed it in, counted from the moment it was sent back.** Hand in an hour before the deadline and you get an hour to correct it; hand in with four minutes to spare and you get four minutes. The time you saved by finishing early is yours to spend on the fix, which is the point — nobody is better off for having cut it fine. So a task due at 6:00 PM, handed in at 5:00 PM and sent back at 5:45 PM, is due one hour after 5:45 PM, at 6:45 PM — and yes, that can land later than the original 6:00 PM, because rework is new work and is measured against the date it was given. The time is OFFICE time, not clock time, so if the office closes at 6:00 PM the remainder carries to the next working morning, and a Friday-evening rework falls due on Monday. The new date replaces the old one everywhere, scoring included, and each further round of rework resets it again. What decides this is when you HANDED IT IN, not when the reviewer got round to looking: submit at 5:00 PM against a 6:00 PM deadline and the review only happens at 8:00 PM, and you still get your full hour, running from 8:00 PM. **The one exception is work handed in after its deadline.** That earns no reset: the task comes back with its original deadline, still overdue, and an overdue task will not start its timer — so you have to ask for more time before you can work on the rework. The task says so where the changes are listed, rather than leaving you with a Play button that does nothing. The notification says it too, rather than only the task: work sent back on time reads “you have until 18:14, the time that was left when you handed it in”, and work sent back late reads “the deadline has NOT been reset, because this was handed in late — you will need more time granted before you can start.” **Sending work back now shows the reviewer what else you have on.** Before they commit they see your live work — which task you are on right now, what each one’s priority is, what each is due, and what each becomes once the rework lands. **Choosing a priority for the rework is not currently offered.** The dropdown reads “Leave as it is” and has no other entry, by the owner’s decision on 18 August 2026, so a rework keeps whatever priority the task already had. Nothing behind it was removed — the engine still accepts a priority, applies it to everyone the task is assigned to and re-chains the queue from it — and what follows describes the choice as it works when it is offered. As they change it, the panel shows what that choice would move: “This pushes 2 deadlines out, 1 into another day.” A choice that moves nothing says nothing. Whatever they pick, the rest of your work re-chains behind it, so the numbers you see afterwards are the real ones rather than dates that quietly ignore the rework. Leaving the picker alone keeps the priority the task already had, which is what happens if the reviewer does not care to choose — and if your other deadlines cannot be read, the panel says so and the rework still goes through. What it never changes is HOW LONG you get: that is the leftover-time rule above, and no reviewer sets it by hand. Before this, rework handed back whatever time was left over — deadline minus your submission time — which gave whoever finished earliest the smallest window to redo the work, and gave somebody who scraped in at the last second exactly the same as somebody an hour early.",
