    answer:
      "Play and pause on a task write a real record — pausing is what credits the time you worked, which is why the panel says so above the button. The control answers your press straight away and confirms the write behind it, so you never wait on the network to see the clock move. **A press made while the previous one is still saving is held, not lost.** Pausing flips the display the instant you confirm it, while the record behind it is still being written — so pressing Resume a second later landed in that gap and did nothing at all, and the timer appeared to start only when you pressed a second time. That press is now remembered and runs the moment the previous write finishes, so one press is enough and Resume responds the first time. Only the most recent press is held: jabbing the button does not queue a row of starts and stops, and a pause you have already confirmed is not asked about again when it runs. If that write does not come back within about twelve seconds the control stops assuming it worked: the display reverts to what the server actually holds and says “That did not reach the server in time. The timer is showing what the server holds — try again.” That message is the honest one to get — it means the press has not been confirmed, rather than the button having quietly stopped responding. **It clears itself if the write lands late, and you should not press again while it is showing a running clock.** Giving up waiting is not the same as the write having failed: it often arrives a moment after the control stopped waiting for it, and when it does the message goes on its own and the clock carries on. It used to stay until your next successful press, so a timer that was genuinely running sat underneath a warning telling you to try again — and trying again paused the timer you had just started, which is why resuming could look like it never worked. If the message is still on screen while the button still offers to start, the press really did not land and pressing again is the right thing to do. Before this, a write that never came back left the button dropping every later press with nothing on screen to explain it, and left the task page reading “Paused” while the timer in the top bar carried on counting, because the two were reading different things. The timer in the top bar shows whichever task is running and re-checks the engine every twenty seconds and whenever you come back to the tab, so if a session is stopped by something other than your own press — a pause you made on another device, for instance — the pill corrects itself rather than counting on. The figure on the task page and the figure in the top bar are the same session and should agree; if they ever do not, the task page is the one reading the live record. **Pausing asks you to confirm**, because pausing is not just a display change: it closes the run and writes the record that credits your time. The deliberate press asks “Pause the timer?” and nothing is written until you say yes; the automatic pauses — going offline, taking a break, starting another task — never ask, since nobody is at the screen for them. **Once the deadline has passed the timer stops and cannot be started.** The panel reads Blocked, shows how long ago the deadline went, and says what to do: ask for more time. Nothing you already worked is taken away — the banked figure stays on screen — and the block is worked out from the task’s current deadline rather than stored anywhere, so the moment more time is approved the timer is usable again with nothing to switch back on. Only the timer is blocked: you can still submit, chat, and ask for the extension itself. **Reloading the page does not stop your timer.** A running session lives on the server, not in the tab, so refreshing, opening the task in a second tab, navigating away and back, or closing the laptop lid and returning all pick the same run up where it was — the clock carries on from when you actually pressed play, not from when the page loaded. **The timer does not stop itself, and a quiet spell does not stop it either.** Only four things stop a running clock: your own press, going offline or on a break, starting a different task, and signing out. A backgrounded tab is none of them. Browsers slow down the checks a hidden tab makes — and stop them entirely on a sleeping laptop or an occluded window — and Cowork used to read that silence as you having left, so people working in another window came back to a stopped timer they had to start again. It no longer does. When the checks resume after a quiet spell, the untracked minutes are discarded and the clock carries on running: the gap is not paid for, because nothing recorded you working through it, but you are not made to press Start again for a break you never took. What you will see is the elapsed figure having advanced by less than the wall clock, which is the gap being left out rather than time going missing. **The figure only ever moves forward.** While a gap is still being worked out the clock can hold still for a while, but it does not drop: what is on screen is the same figure being credited, so there is nothing for the record to correct it to afterwards. A clock that jumps backwards is a fault worth reporting. If a refresh does leave the clock paused, that is a fault worth reporting rather than how it is meant to work.",
