    answer:
      "The bar across the top of Overview is your current work, and it is always there — it does not come and go depending on whether you have anything on. It shows one task at a time, and the P-tag at its left is a picker: your work is ordered by priority, so P1 is what you are on and P2, P3 and the rest are behind it. Choosing another one only changes what the bar is showing. It does not reorder anything, nothing is written, and nobody is told. The bar reads in one of three ways. **With nothing assigned to you** it says so — \"Nothing assigned to you\" — with a link through to Tasks. That is the whole message: an empty queue is a good state, not a problem to fix. It used to vanish entirely in this case, which left a gap where a fixed part of the page should be and no way to tell \"you have nothing\" from \"something is broken\". **With a task you have not started** the title sits in a rounded inset, and resting on it opens the brief: the task's reference, the project and due date, its description, and the list of what has to be true for it to count as complete, ticked items struck through. That is there so \"what am I actually being asked to do\" is answerable without leaving the page and losing your place — the row itself carries no captions, so what they used to say lives in this panel and in the timer's. To the right, **Task deadline** names when the work is expected by — the day and the time of day, in IST, because a deadline is an instant and a bare date leaves a whole working day of doubt about which instant it means. It is the committed deadline where one has been agreed, and otherwise the completion time the budget itself works out to, which is the same figure the task page calls \"Expected completion\". Beside it the time budget is a stepper in hours and minutes: the minutes move in fives and roll into the hour, so stepping up from 55 gives you the next hour at 0 rather than stopping at 59. Leave the figure alone and the button beside it reads **Approve task** — pressing it accepts the time as it stands and takes the task on, in one press rather than an approval followed by a separate receipt to confirm. Change the figure and the same button becomes **Send for approval**, because a different number is a counter-proposal and the other side has to agree to it. **The reason is asked for when you press that button, not before.** Pressing **Send for approval** opens a small panel under it with a box for why, and **Cancel** and **Send**. Where your change refuses the window your assignor first offered, **Send** stays greyed out until you write something: a counter-proposal reaching somebody with a reason nobody typed is worse than no reason at all. Where the negotiation is already open the note is optional, and the figure can go on its own. Putting the stepper back where it was closes the panel and withdraws the request — changing a number and asking for it are two separate acts. There is no clock at any of this, deliberately: no work session exists yet, and a timer here would be counting work nobody has agreed to do, which is also why the figure reads budget rather than left. If the step is not a time one at all — a deadline to propose — the button says so and opens the task. If it belongs to somebody else entirely, the bar states who it is waiting on and offers no button, since pressing one would be refused. **Approving turns that half of the bar into the timer**, in place, ready to start. **With a task under way** it shows the clock and Submit, with Submit in grey to the clock's left — and **Task deadline** stays exactly where it was, because when the work is due does not stop mattering the moment you accept it. **The clock always shows a time, never a word.** Before the first press it reads 00:00:00 rather than \"Start\"; the play triangle beside it is what says it can be started. That way the slot means the same thing at every point instead of showing a verb until you press it and a figure afterwards. Resting on the clock opens how much of the budget is left and a **Request more time** button. **That request is made on the bar, in the same shape the time was first agreed in**: a stepper for how much more on top of what is already agreed, a box for why, and Send. It says what total the addition would make, so there is no guessing whether a figure means \"more\" or \"altogether\". It used to be a link onto the task's deadline page — a different screen and a different form for the identical negotiation you had already been through on that row. Your manager answers, and you confirm whatever they grant, exactly as when the request is made from the task page. The clock turns amber once you are over the budget or past the deadline, and the hover reads \"Over by\" instead of \"left\". **Once the deadline has passed the clock stays on the row**, amber, showing the time already banked; it used to be replaced by a **Blocked** pill, which took the time off the bar at the moment people most wanted to see it. The figure does not move, because nothing accrues past a missed deadline — that is the rule, not a display quirk — and hovering says so and offers the way out. This is the same session the task page shows; if the two ever disagree, the task page is the one reading the live record.",
