    answer:
      "Notifications arrive for the things somebody else does that change your work. On your score: whenever points are deducted from your record, naming the rule applied, the amount, who applied it and their reason — and when points are credited for an approved goal component. If you ask for a recheck of a deduction, the person who applied it and your manager are told; when they decide it, you are told the outcome and their note, whether it was upheld or reversed. On tasks: assigned to you, confirmed, declined, started, moved into a project, details edited, sent back to draft, chat and daily reports, submitted for review, approved, sent back for rework, deleted, and both cross-department approval steps. On time: a deadline proposed, approved, rejected, countered or answered, a deadline extension requested or decided, more time on a task budget requested or decided, a timer estimate you set being accepted or rejected, and an automatic extension from a priority change. Anything waiting on a decision reaches the person who has to make it, and the answer reaches the person who asked — so nobody has to keep opening a task to find out whether it has been looked at. On your queue: whenever a manager reorders your work — reordering it yourself notifies nobody, since you did it. Elsewhere: meetings scheduled, started, changed or cancelled; group and direct messages; being added to or removed from a group, made or unmade a group admin, or a group you are in being renamed; a document or sheet shared with you, your role on it changed, your access removed, or one you share being renamed or deleted; material requests and their approvals, rejections, cancellations and conversations; and your role, department, Cowork ID or password being changed by an administrator. Changing your own email or password sends nothing, because you were the one who did it. Notifications also reach your phone or desktop as a push. The first time you sign in, Cowork asks once — a bar at the top of the page offering to turn them on — and pressing Turn on brings up your browser's own confirmation. Allowing is remembered by the browser permanently, so you are never asked again on that device and every later sign-in registers it silently. Pressing Not now is remembered too, and the bar does not come back; the control on the notifications page turns them on whenever you change your mind. Each device registers separately, so allowing it on your laptop does not cover your phone and you will be asked once there as well. Tapping a push opens the same place the in-app notification does. If your phone is off, asleep or has no signal when something happens, the push waits and arrives when you are back online, for up to a day. On an iPhone or iPad you have to add Cowork to your Home Screen first — Apple only delivers notifications to a web app that has been installed, so allowing them in a normal Safari tab does nothing. Push is an extra, not the record — if you refuse it, or your browser does not support it, or it is dropped somewhere between us and your phone, everything still arrives in the app exactly as before. Opening a notification takes you to what it is about — the task, the meeting, the document, the conversation — and marks it read on the way. Notifications with nowhere to go, such as a password reset, have no link. You can clear one at a time with Mark read, or the whole list with Mark all read. A notification you have already read stays in the list; the unread filter is what hides it.",
