    answer:
      "Workspace, then Sheets. They are listed the same way documents are, and Ctrl K opens the same command palette. **New sheet opens a blank one that is not in the list yet.** It joins the list the moment there is something to keep — the first cell you type, or a second tab — so that merely opening New sheet and looking at it does not leave an empty \"Untitled sheet\" behind every time. If you want an empty one kept anyway, **File ▸ Save** makes the record there and then; that is what Save is for, and it works on a sheet you have not typed in. Naming a sheet before it has been kept does not create it on its own — the name is held and the sheet is stored under it whenever it is stored — so a sheet you name and then abandon is not in the list, and one you name and save is, under the name you gave it. The chip beside the title says where the save is up to: Saving…, Saved, or the reason it could not. Leaving a sheet — the ← back to the list — keeps everything, including an edit made in the last second or two: the pending save is sent on the way out. Closing the whole tab while a save is still running asks before leaving, so the only way to lose that last edit is to tell the browser to leave anyway. A sheet opens on the whole screen — there is no half-width version, because a grid in half a window shows about six columns — and the ← at the top left goes back to the list. Above the grid there is a formula bar showing the selected cell or range, and a formatting toolbar: font and size, bold, italic, underline and strikethrough, text and fill colour, borders, alignment and wrapping, indent, number formats including currency and percent, decimal places, and Merge cells (Unmerge once a merged cell is selected — merging is display-only, so the cells being merged keep whatever they held, only the top-left one shows). Format Painter copies one cell's whole formatting — click it, then click the cell that should look the same; it replaces that cell's formatting entirely, clearing anything it had that the copied cell didn't. Borders opens a menu of presets over the current selection — all borders, outside borders, or just one side — rather than drawing lines one cell at a time. **Cut does not empty the cells straight away** — the same as Excel and Google Sheets: Ctrl+X marks the cells with a moving dashed border and the contents move when you paste, so a cut you never paste destroys nothing. Escape dismisses a pending cut (the border stops and nothing will be cleared), and so does editing any cell, inserting or deleting rows or columns, or switching sheets — after any of those, pasting places plain values and no longer clears the cut cells. A copy's border is a still dashed line; only a cut's border moves. Formulas start with = and are calculated as you type; alongside the usual arithmetic, text joins with the & operator — =A1&\" \"&B1 — the same way CONCATENATE does. As you type a function name a list of matching functions appears — each with its arguments and a one-line description of what it does and an example — and Tab or Enter drops the highlighted one in; once you are inside a function's brackets a helper shows that function's signature with the argument you are on in bold. A cell that cannot be worked out says why rather than showing a code. Right-clicking a cell gives cut, copy, paste, fill down and right, clearing contents or formatting separately, and inserting or deleting rows and columns — it acts on whatever rows or columns the current selection spans (select three rows first to insert three), or on the active cell's own row/column if nothing is selected. A row or column can be resized by dragging the thin handle on its header's edge — the bottom edge of a row number, the right edge of a column letter — which shows a guide line while dragging and applies the new size on release. Copying an image (a screenshot, a photo from another app) and pasting it into a cell puts the picture there, filling that one cell, in place of whatever it held — it replaces text the way any paste does, and Delete/Backspace clears it like any other cell content. A range plus the chart control inserts a chart onto the sheet as a movable object — column, bar, line, area, pie, doughnut, scatter or combo, a different thing from an image pasted into a cell: a chart floats free over the grid, a pasted image lives inside the one cell it was dropped on. Conditional formatting is a panel rather than a menu item, so the rules on a range can be read together. A tab strip along the bottom holds every sheet in the workbook, up to thirty-two: click a tab to switch to it, double-click or use its pencil icon to rename it, the swatch gives it a colour, and its × removes it — the last remaining sheet can't be removed, and removing any other asks you to confirm first, since it takes its cells, formatting and charts with it. One sheet's formula can reference another by name, written as SheetName!A1. The File button (the download-shaped icon in the header) holds “Save as a copy,” which duplicates the whole workbook as a new sheet in the list, “Print,” and CSV and Excel (.xlsx) import and export — importing a CSV writes into cells from A1, importing an .xlsx adds each of its sheets as new tabs onto this workbook rather than replacing it, and both exports include every tab, not only the one showing. Sharing, roles and live editing work exactly as they do for a document: the same owners, editors and viewers, and everybody in a sheet sees each other's changes as they are typed.",
